{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "extra_component",
      "components": ["R4", "R5"],
      "pins": ["R4.1", "R4.2", "R5.1", "R5.2"],
      "nets": ["REG_FB", "5V output node (after L1)"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet, R4 (19k6) is connected between the 5 V output node (right of L1, the same node as the top of R5) and the REG_FB node (bottom of R5 / top of R6). It therefore sits in parallel with the upper feedback resistor R5 (6k8). The schematic's own note states 'Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)' and the spec defines this rail as 5 V.",
      "expected": "The feedback divider should be R5 (top) and R6 (bottom) only, giving Vout = 0.8*(1+6800/1300) ≈ 4.98 V ≈ 5 V, as stated in the schematic note and in the spec ('MIC26903YJL-TR synchronous buck regulator steps the battery input down to 5 V', '9A @ 5V max'). Any feedforward element across the top of the divider must be a capacitor (no DC path); a 19k6 DC-conducting resistor there is not part of the intended divider.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet: R5 6k8, R6 1k3, R4 19k6; note 'Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)'"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "A MIC26903YJL-TR synchronous buck regulator steps the battery input down to 5 V ... feedback divider relation 'Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)' ... '9A @ 5V max.'"}
      ],
      "impact": "R4 in parallel with R5 reduces the effective top resistance to 6.8k||19.6k ≈ 4.8 kΩ, so Vout = 0.8*(1+4800/1300) ≈ 3.7 V instead of 5 V. The 5 V rail (servo/PWM bank, GPS module, CH330 USB-UART bridges) would be undervolted and the board would not behave as intended.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board matches the spec almost everywhere (correct MCUs, sensors, addresses, R1=10mΩ shunt, L1=15µH, 3.3 V LDO, and the documented barometer RESET/EOC pin swap is a known bug, not an anomaly). The one deviation supported by the drawing is R4 (19k6) placed across the top of the buck's feedback divider, which contradicts the schematic's own 'Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)' note and the spec's 5 V rail, pulling the 5 V output down to roughly 3.7 V."
}

